    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The floor tiles"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A hall floor is covered by 110 identical triangular tiles. One tile has vertices at (-3, 2), (-1, -1) and (1, 2). How do we find the floor area just from coordinates?"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "area formula",
          "the shoe-lace arrangement",
          "downward products",
          "upward products",
          "collinearity",
          "quadrilateral",
          "positive area"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "The area of a triangle with vertices (x1,y1), (x2,y2), (x3,y3) is half the absolute value of the difference of two sums.\nWrite the vertices column-wise in counter-clockwise order and repeat the first column at the end.\nAdd the products going down to the right: x1 y2 plus x2 y3 plus x3 y1.\nSubtract the products going up: x2 y1 plus x3 y2 plus x1 y3, then take half and keep the answer positive.\nIf the area is zero, the three points are collinear, which lies on a single straight line.\nFor a quadrilateral, split along a diagonal into two triangles, or use the same arrangement with four columns.\nArea is never negative, so we always take the positive value in square units."
        }
      },
